<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahmad Faris al-Shidyaq</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1805–<a href="/years/1887">1887</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Faris al-Shidyaq, Ahmad Faris Shidyaq</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahmad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fari">Faris</a> al-Shidyaq was an Ottoman scholar, writer and journalist from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a>, considered one of the founding fathers of modern Arabic literature. Born a Maronite, he became a Protestant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, where he lived from 1825, and from 1848 worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on an Arabic translation of the Bible, published in 1857. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1855 he published al-Saq 'ala al-saq, a long, digressive novel drawing on his own life. In 1860 he converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, taking the name Ahmad, and in 1861 he founded the Arabic newspaper al-Jawa'ib in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-faris-al-shidyaq.jpg" alt="Ahmad Faris al-Shidyaq" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Khayr al-Din al-Zirikli · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahmad_Faris_al-Shidyaq_-_Al-Alam,_V1,_Page_236.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
